  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so there is zero information about which modality, encoder, profile strategy, or fusion method performs best. The most critical unknown is the performance floor—how well the simplest possible model does. 2) What this tests: config_001 (rna_only, singleprofile, pca_n2000) is the most minimal, interpretable baseline in the search space. It isolates the RNA signal with the most basic linear encoder (PCA to 2000 dims) and a single profile, removing all confounding complexity from learned encoders, multi-profile aggregation, or cross-modal fusion. 3) Why it is the best first use of budget: Establishing this anchor is essential before any comparative conclusion can be drawn. It is also the cheapest configuration to train (no learned image encoder, no transformer aggregation, no fusion), preserving compute for subsequent experiments. Once this floor is known, the next experiments can efficiently test whether (a) image modality adds value, (b) richer RNA encoders (scvi, scgpt) improve over PCA, and (c) multimodal fusion outperforms either single modality—each a high-uncertainty, high-impact design question that this baseline makes interpretable."
